  "introText": "A money market account is a bank deposit account covered by [Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC)](https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/understanding-deposit-insurance/) insurance within limits, and a money market fund is a mutual fund with no FDIC coverage. At ModernWallet, we write plain-language guides on where to keep cash, and the money market account vs money market fund question starts with the name: the same two words label a bank product and an investment product. The account holds money as a deposit. The fund pools money from many investors and buys short-term debt securities, cash, and cash equivalents, so each share is part ownership of that portfolio.",
