      "question": "What is the difference between a money market account and a money market fund?",
      "answer": "A money market account is a deposit account at a bank, and a money market fund is a mutual fund that invests in liquid, short-term debt securities, cash, and cash equivalents. The account is covered by FDIC insurance up to $250,000 per depositor, per FDIC-insured bank, per ownership category. The fund is not covered, because FDIC insurance applies to deposits and a fund share is part ownership of a portfolio. The account pays a variable rate, while the fund pays dividends that generally reflect short-term interest rates."
